last week a huge rust twitch rivals  tournament involving north american and  spanish streamers imploded in a big way  the spanish streamers accused twitch of  rigging the game in the north americans  favor while they themselves were accused  of inciting racism streamers like  disguise toast sqc and shroud have now  weighed in on what might be one of the  most insane twitch stories of the year  here's how it happened  all right first off rust is a  multiplayer survival game where players  gather resources craft weapons build  bases and fight each other in an open  world on persistent servers it sounds  simple but it's absolutely captivated  twitch for the past couple of years  which has culminated in this recent  twitch rivals event and it was massive  there were 14 north american streamers  versus 40 streamers from spain and latin  america team north america led by  disguise toast faced off against the  spanish-speaking team led by alex by 11.  team north america also featured big  streamers like xqc tarik and shroud the  battle was to take place on vanilla rust  servers for 24 hours starting in the  primitive era teams could earn points by  eliminating players and cashing in their  dog tags hackable crates neutral bases  and npcs could also drop dog tags the  team with the most dog tags at the end  would win simple in the beginning the  tournament delivered the typically  chaotic content that russ fans are  familiar with he seems to have done more  damage to the actual elliot  yeah so they're gonna fall back real  quick because  thankfully they can't be shot at here  you can just hear the bottom right in  spanish right now but this is a safe  zone so if anyone tries  me i ram you but as the tournament  progressed things took a negative twist  early on the na team destroyed the  spanish team's twig base which went  against the rule of not touching  buildings in your opponent's base square  admins were called in later that day the  spanish hit na back hard destroying a  lot of bags and loot in their base  because north america had left a lot of  their doors open admins were once again  called in and attempted to find a middle  ground between the two but this sort of  squabbling really came to a head when  disguised toast north america's team  captain was accused by the spanish of  cheating it all stemmed from one clip in  particular in which an mp5 appeared to  spawn out of toast's body and land  behind him  oh do not step into bush thank you  mysterious admin guns spawning out of  thin air and players then thanking the  admins for them definitely raised a few  eyebrows in the spanish community toast  was accused of cheating and the admins  of helping the north american side one  spanish streamer riccotti said there was  an embarrassing lack of respect and that  the tournament was disastrously  organized as the clip began to rack up  thousands of views toast pulled it up on  his stream to explain his side of things  it's in my hand  what am i gonna do with two mp5s and you  know what's the worst part  look at the top left  the event was over  25k to 5k  this was after the spanish team  announced they were quitting that's the  thing people are complaining about an  out of context clip which actually gives  context in the clip as well because you  can see the score on the top left and  you can see the gun in my hand so even  out of contacts  there was  contacts and that was still enough to  get them to accuse the admins of  cheating now that might seem like a  perfectly reasonable explanation right  the game was over he already had that  particular gun in his hands so there was  no way that this could have affected the  outcome of the tournament which again  was already over right but that wasn't  the only accusation that came from the  spanish side in a twitter thread the  spanish captain listed a number of  issues that he had with the tournament  these included the north american  streamers crafting pistols and grenades  when they should have only had primitive  tech that they refused to return items  to them and that they duplicated the  spanish team's heli later that day  twitch rivals announced something that  many people had seen coming by this  point they said on twitter that the  tournament was ending early and that  team disguised toast would be taking  home the win it was a forfeit from the  spanish side and the victory didn't  taste too sweet for a lot of north  american streamers for a lot of them  they felt the whole thing was  kind of bull we're not having a meeting  streaming it or telling people to quit  pretty much  good for them  because they're getting ready they think  we're being unfair again  unfair that we're so good what do you  think these people will be invited to no  and no i hope like  you're just like no no no the event  that's a really bad look for  all those involved  no i don't think they'll be invited  again maybe some of them the people that  weren't doing that people that actually  cared about it sure i think they should  get invited back damn that is a poor  sportsmanship for sure from them holy  kind of embarrassing honestly  yeah but  whatever whatever but they were far  darker accusations that have been  building since day two far worse than  just time wasting some north american  streamers such as mendo called out  spanish viewers for making racist  remarks in the north american streamers  chats  [Music]  a day after the event ended toast went  on stream to talk about the whole  situation and go through some unbanned  requests it was then that he came across  one particularly disturbing comment it  is not known if you are a gringo  chinese or korean  you don't even have a garbage identity  well i can understand why he's confused  because i am both chinese and korean  according to my most recent dna test  toast handled the whole situation  incredibly gracefully given the  circumstances as the team captain he  took the brunt of a lot of this and  don't forget he was the one who  initially approached twitch with the  idea for this particular event even  though he got some pretty nasty comments  thrown his way he still said there were  things that could be improved for next  time to make the spanish streamers feel  like the environment is more fair  because in his own words if there's no  content everybody loses and it's in  twitch's best interest to make sure that  this kind of thing doesn't happen too  one other thing that toast talked about  was the amount of money that twitch had  put into this event there was a hundred  thousand dollars in the price pool and  another hundred thousand into production  to put it simply they don't want teams  just dropping out early from tournaments  that they put almost a quarter of a  million dollars into that's just bad  business as they said in their twitter  post they're going to clarify rules in  the future to prevent confusion and also  reevaluate their format two issues that  seem to be the roots of a lot of the  problems it's difficult with a game like  rust because a lot of these rules are  hard to enforce rules like not going  more than two doors deep into a person's  base can only really be kept by a  gentleman's agreement unless you want to  retroactively vod review every streamer  but one issue that looms larger than the  rest are the accusations of racism  twitch hasn't addressed these directly  but for many people it tainted what was  otherwise a pretty entertaining twitch  rivals  [Music]  you
 

